How to Apply OSEL to Your Software
----------------------------------

To apply OSEL Version 0.1 to your software, include the full license text
in a file named LICENSE in the root directory of your project.

Add the following notice at the top of each source code file:

    Copyright (c) <year> <copyright holder>

    This software is released under the Open Source Evolution License
    (OSEL), Version 0.1. That license permits Recipients, subject to
    specified conditions, to evolve the license into an OSI-Approved
    Compatible License that imposes additional obligations. The complete
    license text is available in the LICENSE file in the project root
    directory.

If you distribute a new version and wish to exercise the replacement
right under Section 5, you must create a file named
LICENSE-REPLACEMENT.md in the root directory of the software. That file
must contain the information required by Section 5.1(c). See
examples/LICENSE-REPLACEMENT.md for a template.

Compliance and the Open Source Definition
-----------------------------------------

OSEL is designed to be consistent with the Open Source Definition. The
most frequently discussed issue is whether the replacement right in
Section 5 conflicts with OSD Criterion 3.

OSD Criterion 3 states that a license must allow modifications and
derived works to be distributed under the same terms as the license of
the original software. It does not require that a license must require
distribution under the same terms. The word "allow" is not the same as
"require."

OSEL preserves the recipient's ability to distribute derivative works
under OSEL itself. Section 2(d) grants the right to distribute the
software and derivative works. The replacement right in Section 5 is an
additional option; it does not remove the option to use OSEL.

The replacement right is therefore intended to expand, not reduce, the
freedoms available to recipients.

The more significant compliance questions concern OSD Criteria 5 and 6,
which prohibit discrimination against persons, groups, and fields of
endeavor. OSEL addresses these concerns through Section 1.7(c), which
limits additional obligations to a closed list of categories, and through
Section 5.3(c), which expressly prohibits replacement with a license that
adds restrictions on field of use, users, or business models.

For a more detailed discussion, see docs/compliance/osd-checklist.md.
That document is a draft analysis, not a legal opinion.

Relationship to Other Licenses
------------------------------

OSEL is not the same as MIT, Apache-2.0, GPL-3.0, EUPL-1.2, or MPL-2.0.

OSEL shares with MIT a permissive baseline. It adds a patent grant, a
contributor grant, and the replacement mechanism in Section 5.

OSEL shares with Apache-2.0 a patent grant and patent retaliation
termination. The patent grant in Section 3.1 is limited to patent claims
that are necessarily infringed by the contribution itself or by the
combination of the contribution with the software.

OSEL shares with EUPL-1.2 and MPL-2.0 the idea that a downstream
recipient may, under certain conditions, distribute a new version under
another approved license. Unlike EUPL-1.2 and MPL-2.0, OSEL does not use a
fixed list of compatible licenses. Instead, Section 1.7 anchors
compatibility in the OSI approval process and in the criteria set out in
Section 1.7(c).

This difference is intentional. It is also one of the issues that may
attract scrutiny if OSEL is submitted for OSI certification.

Contributing
------------

Contributions to the OSEL specification are welcome.

Before contributing, please read CONTRIBUTING.md.

In summary:

1. Open an issue before making a substantial change to the license text.
2. Use the issue templates where available.
3. Keep changes focused and explain the reason for each change.
4. Be prepared to discuss how a proposed change affects compliance with
   the Open Source Definition.
5. By contributing, you agree that your contribution may be used,
   modified, and distributed under the terms applicable to this
   repository's documentation.

This repository's documentation is licensed separately from the OSEL
license text itself. See the LICENSE file for details. The OSEL license
text in LICENSES/OSEL-0.1.md is dedicated to the public domain under
CC0 1.0, so that anyone may copy, adopt, and modify it freely.

Frequently Asked Questions
--------------------------

Q: Is OSEL an OSI-approved license?
A: No. OSEL Version 0.1 is a working draft. It has not been submitted to
   OSI for certification.

Q: Can I use OSEL for my project now?
A: You may use the draft text if you wish, but you should review it
   carefully. Because it is a draft, its terms may change in later
   versions. If you need a stable, well-known license, consider using an
   existing OSI-approved license instead.

Q: Does the replacement right violate OSD Criterion 3?
A: The draft takes the position that it does not. OSD Criterion 3
   requires that distribution under the same terms be allowed, not that
   it be required. OSEL allows distribution under OSEL itself. The
   replacement right adds an option; it does not remove that option.

Q: Can a recipient replace OSEL with MIT or BSD?
A: No. Section 5.3(b) prohibits replacement with a license that imposes
   obligations fewer than or equal to those of OSEL. MIT and BSD are
   less restrictive than OSEL and therefore do not qualify as Compatible
   Licenses.

Q: Can a recipient replace OSEL with GPL-3.0?
A: GPL-3.0 is an OSI-approved license and imposes additional obligations.
   Whether it qualifies as a Compatible License depends on the analysis
   under Section 1.7. This is an open question in the draft and is
   discussed in docs/compatibility/known-compatible-licenses.md.

Q: Does the replacement right affect the patent license?
A: Section 3.4 states that the patent license granted under Section 3.1
   does not terminate or shrink merely because a recipient exercises the
   replacement right. However, the retaliation termination clause in
   Section 3.2 continues to apply. If the target license provides an
   equivalent or stronger patent license, the recipient may elect to
   apply the target license's patent provisions instead.

Q: Is OSEL legal advice?
A: No. Nothing in this repository is legal advice. If you intend to use
   OSEL in a commercial or high-risk context, consult a qualified lawyer.

